From 37102ed2bb8c33fdd38b89ef2bc85ba635dc70ef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SorinS <ssuciu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 23:08:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] -g, --debug-info: build a program for stepping its Goose
 source

--debug gives the overflow, range and tag checks and C debug information,
but a debugger then steps the generated C: its lines, its temporaries,
and functions Goose inlined into their callers. -g (or --debug-info), the
flag C compilers, rustc, swiftc and GHC use for debug information, gives
--debug and builds the C for a debugger:

- Every line of a function's C is preceded by a #line naming the Goose
  line it came from: a statement's, a match arm's, a block's or a
  function's tail value's, the declaration's for the prologue. One
  directive per C line, not per statement, since after `#line N` the
  following lines count on as N+1, N+2; lines put in after the body was
  written (hoisted declarations, a loop's cached top) get the directive
  repeated when the C is assembled (ResetLines). File names are absolute.
  Each function ends with a #line back to the .c file at its true line,
  so the runtime and helpers that follow are not charged to Goose.
- A debugger puts a breakpoint location in every lexical block a line's
  code passes through, and a C block that declares a variable is one:
  every declaration, scalars, pointers and for counters included, is
  hoisted to the function's opening brace, so a Goose line is one
  location and a loop's body stops each time round.
- No inlining unless an -O asks for it, so every function keeps its
  frame; folding and propagation run as at every level.
- No resizable's count is cached: every push writes the header, so the
  array is shown as it is from any frame while a loop grows it.

Without -g nothing changes, and --debug alone gives the C it gave.

Test: with LLDB, cdb and gdb, on macOS arm64, Windows x64 (MSVC, PDB)
and Linux x86_64 (gcc and clang): a breakpoint on a Goose line binds and
stops there, once a line and each time round a loop; a function keeps
its frame; the call stack names .goose files and lines.
